Final release audit: 2026-11-05

Scope and findings
  Reviewed the prediction, plotting-grid, contrast-slope, Johnson-Neyman, and
  ordinary-model refitting paths against their documented contracts and tests.
  No additional numerical discrepancy was found in those reviewed paths.
  This is a focused release audit, not a proof of every supported model case.

  One pre-existing jtools defect was reproduced: two_sided(y ~ x) errored
  because debugging code treated the base debug function as a logical flag.
  Removed the debugging code, retained the original formula classification,
  and added seven assertions for formulas, transformations, and Formula objects.
  Formula is now a test-only Suggests dependency. The fix is in the working
  JacobTools tree and the isolated 2.3.2 maintenance patch.

  Added the missing usage entry for the scale_lm alias. Interactions release
  notes now identify panelr >= 1.1.0 and jtools >= 2.3.2 explicitly.
  No panelr or interactions executable code changed in this final audit.

Interpretation contract
  Panel histories and hypothetical plotting grids are separate prediction paths.
  Default within-person axes and moderator values use deviations in original
  predictor units, including standardized fits. The outcome is not demeaned.
  Original-scale plots hold reference person/interaction means fixed and label
  them. Slopes differentiate that same conditional fixed-effect prediction,
  using the full fitted covariance, not a refit or recomputed panel history.
  Non-identity links yield link-scale slopes; GEE uses its fitted covariance.
  Gaussian mixed-model t tests use a stated residual-df approximation, not
  Satterthwaite/Kenward-Roger. Unsupported cases error explicitly. Johnson-Neyman
  intervals are pointwise. Documentation and tests make these limits explicit.

